\pard\li175 \cf1 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} 123IITU}
\b Paracelsus\plain\fs20\cf1 
\par 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} DIROL}
Dirol
\par This is an example of a planet that is probably a captured asteroid. It is dense enough to exert a Terra-class gravitational field. Most of Dirol\'92s surface is covered with ice, probably composed of frozen gases. Varied elevations on Dirol\'92s glaciers provide some strategic cover, though movement on ice is slower and hinders targeting.
\par Gravity: 100%; Atmosphere: None; life threatening; EM Field: Variant; effects on shields will be greatly amplified.
\par \pard\li175 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} BERAN}
Beran
\par This planet is an anomaly. Its orbit has an average radius of 300 million miles, yet it is a completely developed Terra class world with atmosphere and vegetation. There is evidence to suggest that Beran and Brell my be terraformed planets.
\par Changes in elevation on Beran are gradual and provide little obstructive cover. There are enough trees and boulders, however, to provide some defense. Hercs should move well over hard rock, while icy terrain and swamps provide poor fighting conditions.
\par \pard\li175 Gravity: 93%; Atmosphere: Normal, life supporting; EM Field: Significantly polarized; poses no danger and may have beneficial effects on Bioderm Stability and skill implants.
\par \i 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} 124RAM_}
Mardalla\plain\fs20\cf1 
\par Mardalla is the larger of Beran\'92s two moons and younger by 500 million years. This moon\'92s age may be insufficient for the generation of complex ore deposits. Its size and an unusually dense core enable it to retain a hospitable gravity, an electromagnetic field, and even a minimal atmosphere. Because Mardalla is heavily composed of toxic metallic compounds, its atmosphere would soon poison a Bioderm that is not protected by a life support system. Its high metallic content is also responsible for terrain discoloration.
\par \pard\li175 Gravity: 50%; Atmosphere: Poisonous, life threatening; EM Field: Normal
\par \i 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} TIRITH}
Tirith\plain\fs20\cf1 
\par Tirith is smaller and older than its neighbor moon, Mardalla. This moon\'92s heavily cratered surface attests to its advanced age.  It has been proposed that Tirith is actually a meteor captured by Beran\'92s gravity. In this event, the potential exists for a greater number of ore deposits being present than on a normal moon.
\par Extremely gradual changes in elevation offer few strategic opportunities. Huge craters may provide some defense, but will upset targeting. Smooth terrain affords easier movement, while jagged or rough terrain hinders movement and targeting ability.
\par \pard\li175 Gravity: 37%; Atmosphere: None; life threatening; EM Field: systemsVariant; Greatly accelerates Bioderm aging.
\par 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} BRELL}
Brell
\par Brell is an even greater mystery than its neighbor, Beran. Brell is over 500 million miles from the sun, yet it hosts an even more lush ecology than Beran. The mountainous jungle terrain of this planet is fed by a network of rivers. Early data concerning Brell indicated a planet covered by desert. If this is true, Brell may have been terraformed by the Cybrids. Varied elevations on Brell provide some degree of cover. Much of the larger plant life provides some defense as well. Water is difficult to move through and is a defensive disadvantage. Jagged terrain hinders movement and targeting ability.
\par \pard\li175 Gravity: 90%; Atmosphere: Normal, life supporting; EM Field: Significantly polarized; implantgreatly enhances sensors.
\par \i 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} ORA}
Ora\plain\fs20\cf1 
\par Former site of the Mox Tarsus mining colony. Its erratic terrain and heavy Cybrid infestation have made this moon difficult to mine. 
\par Varied elevations on Ora provide some strategic cover. Smoother ground allows easier movement. Rough or jagged terrain is more difficult to cross and upsets targeting.
\par It has half the gravity of a Terra class planet. Gravity: 52%; Atmosphere: None; life threatening; EM Field: Normal
\par \pard\li175 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} PARSUS}
Parsus
\par The same tri-nitrogen compound responsible for the brilliant terrain discoloration on Parsus causes a buildup of inert gases in Bioderm respiratory tissues. This would eventually create a condition similar to nitrogen narcosis and quickly asphyxiate a Bioderm operating without minimal life support.
\par Though much of the topography on Parsus is made up of gradual changes in elevation, there are enough cliffs to provide adequate defense. Smoother ground allows easier movement. Rough or jagged terrain is more difficult to cross and upsets targeting.
\par \pard\li175 Gravity: 117%; Atmosphere: Highly poisonous; life threatening; EM Field: Normal
\par 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} IONIS}
\b Ionis\plain\fs20\cf1 
\par 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} 3KKL_LL}
Hyperion
\par Continuous volcanic activity, combined with Hyperion\'92s proximity to the star Ionis, keep the planet's surface temperature high enough to quickly kill a Bioderm without minimal Life Support.
\par Hyperion\'92s topography provides some degree of cover. Many of the larger cinder cones can be used defensively as well.
\par Gravity: 89%; Atmosphere: Highly poisonous; life threatening; EM Field: systemsVariable; greatly accelerates Bioderm aging.
\par \pard\li175 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} PRAAB}
Praab
\par Praab is a tiny jungle planet that should be covered in ice. The presence of an electromagnetic field with an inhibiting wave signature greatly hindered initial analysis.
\par Praab\'92s mountainous terrain offers some strategic elements, though changes in elevation are too gradual to offer much cover. Trees and boulders offer some protection, and a series of bizarre monoliths found only on Praab are unusually beneficial.
\par Gravity: 82%; Atmosphere: Normal; life supporting; EM Field: The electromagnetic field surrounding this planet displays an inhibiting wave signature that will constrict any electronflux capacitor, and render ELF weapons useless.
\par \pard\li175 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} SALIKH}
Salikh
\par Salikh may have lost any breathable atmosphere it once had during its initial formation. Any water remaining would be at the sub-microscopic level. Changes in elevation on Salikh are greater than they appear. Mesas and spires on this world provide excellent strategic opportunities.
\par Gravity: 70%; Atmosphere: None; life threatening; EM Field: The atmosphere of this world is densely saturated with particulate matter, which prevents the firing mechanisms of cannons and other ballistic weapons from operating.
\par \pard\li175 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} DORN}
Dorn
\par The planet Dorn was most likely a Terra-class world at one time. Oxidation and nitrate depletion have destroyed any native life, though Dorn\'92s atmosphere will remain breathable for several millennia. Sudden changes in elevation may be the result of internal instability. In fact, the elevation extremes have earned Dorn the nickname \'93Maze World.\'94 These natural formations provide strategic advantages. Dead trees and boulders could also be used defensively, if needed. Polarized amber, found only on Dorn, may somehow aid weapon performance.
\par \pard\li175 Gravity: 150%; Atmosphere: Normal; life supporting; EM Field: Significantly polarized; implantgreatly enhances sensors.\i 
\par 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} ODON}
Odon
\par \plain\fs20\cf1 Odon meant \'93the golden one\'94 in Newspeech. 
\par Odon\'92s surface is now baked and dry destroying any life it once had. Veins of pyrite-based compound can be found running through it. The deep gouges in the planet\'92s surface could prove to be deathtraps due to the hindering effects of the uneven terrain. Boulders and dead trees may provide enough protection to take control of the surface.
\par \pard\li175 Gravity: 29%; Atmosphere: None; life threatening; EM Field: Densely saturated with particulate matter which prevents the firing mechanisms of cannons and other ballistic weapons from operating.
\par \i 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} DOBO}
Dobo\plain\fs20\cf1 
\par In the \'93Corporate mythos\'94 of the splinter faction that discovered this system, Dobo and Odon were the sons of a great leader, Dorn, who was assassinated by his rivals. The sons avenged his death by destroying all of Dorn\'92s enemies, and then stood vigil at his tomb until his return. In the \'93Newspeech\'94 of that time, the name Dobo meant \'93the aggressive one.\'94
\par \pard\li175 Deep ravines and pits in this world may provide unique cover in some situations.
\par Gravity: 103%; Atmosphere: None; life threatening; EM Field: Acute high-frequency wave distortion which effectively shuts down missile and rocket launch mechanisms.
\par 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} XHIRON}
Xhiron
\par The atmosphere of Xhiron is composed primarily of methane and particulate corrosives. Fissures in the planet\'92s surface continually produce toxic gases, evidence of this world\'92s unstable interior. Xhiron\'92s frozen gas glaciers present excellent opportunities for hit-and-run tactics. Their elevation prevents line of sight possibilities and creates difficult maneuvering conditions.
\par \pard\li175 Gravity: 200%; Atmosphere: Corrosive and/or toxic; life threatening; EM Field: The outer crust of this world is so dense that mining devices will require twice the normal amount of energy to operate.
\par \plain\fs20 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} P9BMG7}
\i\cf1 Basmalt
\par \plain\fs20\cf1 Like Dorn, Basmalt was probably a completely Terra-class world. A shift in this planet\'92s orbit resulted in the destruction of any native life. Continuous tectonic activity has resulted in a  mostly uniform topography. Basmalt\'92s atmosphere is considered toxic.
\par \pard\li175 Basmalt\'92s gradual changes in elevation provide little cover. Movement along its rocky ridges proves easier than its sandy floor. Ancient alien artifacts on this planet may have a positive effect on weapons systems.
\par Gravity: 110%; Atmosphere: Corrosive and toxic; life threatening; EM Field: Neutron emissions reveal the presence of a variant electromagnetic field surrounding this planet. The effects of energy-based weapon systems will be greatly impaired.
\par \i 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} TAHN}
Tahn
\par \plain\fs20\cf1 Tahn is a large enough moon to retain a sufficient gravity field and a breathable atmosphere. It has a lush jungle environment dense enough to discourage colonization in some cases. Heavy Cybrid infestation is indicated. This moon is apparently of particular interest to the Cybrids, perhaps because of its electromagnetic field.
\par \pard\li175 Changes in elevation on Tahn are more gradual than they appear. Trees and related plant life are the major defensive elements this world has to offer. Bodies of water are made more difficult to cross by exposed rocks.
\par Gravity: 97%; Atmosphere: Normal; life supporting; EM Field: The electromagnetic field surrounding this world appears to be significantly polarized. implantsSensors will be greatly amplified.
\par \i 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} 1I0.33P}
Kabulov
\par \plain\fs20\cf1 Extreme caution is advised in exploring this moon.  Little is known about Kabulov because of its extremely inhibiting electromagnetic field. It has been speculated that an unknown element exists at this moon\'92s core.
\par \pard\li175 Most of Kabulov appears flat, with occasional unbroken mountain ridges.
\par Gravity: 44%; Atmosphere: Highly poisonous; life threatening; EM Field: Heavy ion signature; dampens the effectiveness of shields, sensors, and weapons systems.
\par \i 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} URSK}
Ursk
\par \plain\fs20\cf1 Ursk is covered in a sheet of frozen methane and trace minerals. Its rugged beauty is offset by an inhibiting electromagnetic field and poisonous atmosphere.
\par Varied elevations on Ursk\'92s glaciers provide some strategic cover, though movement on ice is slower and hinders targeting.
\par \pard\li175 Gravity: 63%; Atmosphere: Corrosive and/or toxic; life threatening; EM Field: Heavy ion signature; dampens the effectiveness of shields, sensors, and energy-based weapons systems.
\par \i 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} MUYNAK}
Muynak
\par \plain\fs20\cf1 In geological terms, Muynak is a recently-formed moon. Shifting plate tectonics continually release lava flows across its surface. Muynak\'92s compact wave electromagnetic field has made analysis difficult.
\par Gradual changes in elevation offer little cover. Many of the larger cinder cones can be used defensively.
\par \pard\li175 Gravity: 33%; Atmosphere: Corrosive and/or toxic; life threatening; EM Field: Compact-wave based; amplifiedAnti-grav systems will not function.
\par \i 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} MORAV}
Morav
\par \plain\fs20\cf1 Morav was seen as an ideal colonization site before Cybrid infestation was discovered. In Newspeech, the name \'93Morav\'94 meant \'93Paradise.\'94
\par Morav is covered by shallow, intersecting rivers. Its gentle terrain offers little protection, although much of the larger plant life could be used as cover.
\par Gravity: 136%; Atmosphere: Normal, life supporting; EM Field: Normal
\par \pard\li175 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} M138}
\b M138\plain\fs20\cf1 
\par 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} A0139P}
Gehenna
\par Robotic probes were destroyed before reaching this planet. Long range spectral analysis suggests a severely corrosive atmosphere due to the presence of unknown compounds. Heavy volcanic activity is also indicated. This planet\'92s code name, as well as its moons, refer to ancient legends of an underworld.
\par Gravity: 213%; Atmosphere: Corrosive and toxic; life threatening; EM Field: weaponsExplosive effects will be greatly dampened.
\par \i 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} CHARON}
Charon
\par \plain\fs20\cf1 Robotic probes were destroyed before reaching this moon. Long-range spectral analysis indicates an extremely corrosive atmosphere due to the presence of unknown compounds
\par \pard\li175 Gravity: 33%; Atmosphere: Highly poisonous; life threatening; EM Field: inoperativeSensors and targeting computers will be significantly impaired.
\par \i 
\par {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} 1OHZV0A}
Acheron\plain\fs20 
\par \cf1 Robotic probes were destroyed before reaching this moon. Long range spectral analysis suggests an extremely corrosive atmosphere and an unusually strong gravitational pull.
\par Gravity: 200%; Atmosphere: Highly poisonous; life threatening; EM Field: The electromagnetic field surrounding this planet indicates a narrow ion signature. This will effectively inoperablrender sensors inoperative.
\par \pard\li175 
\par }